{
  "version": 1,
  "id": "00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000109",
  "title": "Wave Equation",
  "created_at": "2026-01-01T00:00:00Z",
  "updated_at": "2026-01-01T00:00:00Z",
  "shared_cargo_toml": "[profile.release]\nopt-level = 1\nlto = false\ncodegen-units = 16",
  "shared_source": null,
  "cells": [
    {
      "id": "cell_0",
      "order": 0,
      "label": "Introduction",
      "cell_type": "Markdown",
      "source": "# 1D Wave Equation \ud83c\udf0a\n\nThe **wave equation** is one of the fundamental PDEs in physics:\n\n$$\\frac{\\partial^2 u}{\\partial t^2} = c^2 \\, \\frac{\\partial^2 u}{\\partial x^2}$$\n\nIt describes how disturbances propagate through a medium \u2014 vibrating strings, sound waves, electromagnetic radiation, and quantum mechanical wavefunctions.\n\n## This Notebook\n\nWe solve the 1D wave equation using the **finite difference method** and render 200 frames of the wave's evolution as a looping animation:\n\n- **Grid**: 800 spatial points (one per pixel column)\n- **Initial condition**: Two Gaussian pulses at different positions\n- **Boundary**: Fixed ends ($u = 0$) \u2014 pulses reflect with inverted phase\n- **CFL condition**: $c \\cdot \\Delta t / \\Delta x = 0.5 \\le 1$ \u2713 (ensures numerical stability)\n\nWatch the pulses propagate, bounce off the walls, and interfere with each other!",
      "cargo_toml": null
    },
    {
      "id": "cell_1",
      "order": 1,
      "label": "Wave Animation",
      "cell_type": "Code",
      "source": "use ironpad_cell::prelude::*;\n\n// \u2500\u2500 Simulation parameters \u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\n\nconst WIDTH: u32 = 800;\nconst HEIGHT: u32 = 400;\nconst NX: usize = WIDTH as usize;\nconst NUM_FRAMES: usize = 200;\nconst STEPS_PER_FRAME: usize = 12;\nconst FPS: u32 = 30;\n\nlet c = 1.0_f64;\nlet dx = 1.0 / (NX - 1) as f64;\nlet dt = 0.5 * dx / c;\nlet r2 = (c * dt / dx) * (c * dt / dx);\n\n// \u2500\u2500 Rendering \u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\n\nfn render(u: &[f64]) -> Canvas {\n    let mid = HEIGHT as f64 * 0.5;\n    let amp = mid * 0.85;\n\n    Canvas::from_fn(WIDTH, HEIGHT, |px, py| {\n        let val = u[px as usize];\n        let wave_y = mid - val * amp;\n        let fy = py as f64 + 0.5;\n        let dist = (fy - wave_y).abs();\n\n        // Bright cyan wave line\n        if dist < 1.5 {\n            return (100, 220, 255);\n        }\n        // Soft glow around the line\n        if dist < 5.0 {\n            let t = (dist - 1.5) / 3.5;\n            let inv = 1.0 - t * t;\n            return (\n                (15.0 + inv * 85.0) as u8,\n                (15.0 + inv * 205.0) as u8,\n                (30.0 + inv * 225.0) as u8,\n            );\n        }\n        // Gradient fill between wave and center axis\n        let fills_up = val > 0.005 && fy > wave_y && fy < mid;\n        let fills_dn = val < -0.005 && fy < wave_y && fy > mid;\n        if fills_up || fills_dn {\n            let span = (mid - wave_y).abs();\n            let d = if fills_up { fy - wave_y } else { wave_y - fy };\n            let a = 1.0 - (d / span).clamp(0.0, 1.0);\n            let a2 = a * a;\n            return (\n                (15.0 + a2 * 15.0) as u8,\n                (15.0 + a2 * 55.0) as u8,\n                (30.0 + a2 * 130.0) as u8,\n            );\n        }\n        // Thin center axis\n        if (fy - mid).abs() < 0.6 {\n            return (35, 35, 65);\n        }\n        // Dark background\n        (15, 15, 30)\n    })\n}\n\n// \u2500\u2500 Initial condition: two Gaussian pulses \u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\n\nlet sigma = 0.04_f64;\nlet mut u_prev: Vec<f64> = (0..NX)\n    .map(|i| {\n        let x = i as f64 * dx;\n        let p1 = (-(x - 0.3).powi(2) / (2.0 * sigma * sigma)).exp();\n        let p2 = 0.6 * (-(x - 0.7).powi(2) / (2.0 * sigma * sigma)).exp();\n        p1 + p2\n    })\n    .collect();\n\n// First timestep (zero initial velocity: u_t(x, 0) = 0)\nlet mut u_curr = vec![0.0_f64; NX];\nfor i in 1..(NX - 1) {\n    u_curr[i] = u_prev[i]\n        + 0.5 * r2 * (u_prev[i + 1] - 2.0 * u_prev[i] + u_prev[i - 1]);\n}\n\n// \u2500\u2500 Time evolution \u2192 animation frames \u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\n\nlet mut frames = Vec::with_capacity(NUM_FRAMES);\nframes.push(render(&u_prev));\n\nfor _ in 1..NUM_FRAMES {\n    for _ in 0..STEPS_PER_FRAME {\n        let mut u_next = vec![0.0_f64; NX];\n        for i in 1..(NX - 1) {\n            u_next[i] = 2.0 * u_curr[i] - u_prev[i]\n                + r2 * (u_curr[i + 1] - 2.0 * u_curr[i] + u_curr[i - 1]);\n        }\n        // Fixed boundaries: u(0) = u(L) = 0\n        u_prev = u_curr;\n        u_curr = u_next;\n    }\n    frames.push(render(&u_curr));\n}\n\nAnimation::new(frames, FPS)",
      "cargo_toml": null
    },
    {
      "id": "cell_2",
      "order": 2,
      "label": "Wave Physics",
      "cell_type": "Markdown",
      "source": "## Understanding the Physics\n\n### Reflection at Boundaries\n\nWith **fixed boundaries** ($u = 0$ at both ends), waves reflect with **inverted phase** \u2014 the pulse flips upside down. This is because the displacement must remain zero at the boundary.\n\n### Superposition\n\nWhen reflected waves overlap, their displacements add linearly. The wave equation is **linear**, so any sum of solutions is also a solution. This creates intricate interference patterns.\n\n### Standing Waves\n\nAfter multiple reflections, overlapping traveling waves form **standing wave patterns** \u2014 nodes (stationary points) and antinodes (maximum oscillation). The fundamental frequency is $f_1 = c \\,/\\, 2L$.\n\n### Energy Conservation\n\nThe finite difference scheme conserves total wave energy (when CFL $\\le 1$):\n\n$$E = \\frac{1}{2} \\int \\left( u_t^2 + c^2 \\, u_x^2 \\right) dx = \\text{const}$$\n\n### Try Modifying\n\n- Change the pulse positions or widths ($\\sigma$ parameter)\n- Add a third pulse or use a sine wave initial condition\n- Increase `NUM_FRAMES` or `STEPS_PER_FRAME` for longer evolution\n- Try $c = 2.0$ for faster wave propagation",
      "cargo_toml": null
    }
  ],
  "description": "Solve the 1D wave equation and animate propagating wave pulses using the Animation type.",
